---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import BusinessPerspectiveNote from '../../components/BusinessPerspectiveNote.astro';
import ToolNextStep from '../../components/ToolNextStep.astro';
import CareerOptionalityCalculator from '../../components/CareerOptionalityCalculator.astro';

const title = 'Career Optionality Calculator: Can You Change Course Later?';
const description = 'Answer five questions to see how easy it is to change course after a career or course choice, and compare two paths side by side. Free.';
const canonical = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/tools/career-optionality-calculator/';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <Nav activePage="tools" />
  <BusinessPerspectiveNote />
  <main>
    <section class="tool-hero container"><p class="eyebrow">FREE DECISION WORKSHEET</p><h1>Career Optionality <span>Calculator</span></h1><p class="lead">Check how many choices a path keeps open before you make an expensive or hard-to-undo commitment. Try it for two paths and compare them in one table.</p><p class="tool-limits">A thinking aid, not a promise about jobs, pay or demand.</p></section>
    <section class="container tool-shell"><CareerOptionalityCalculator /></section>
    <section class="container tool-explainer"><h2>What optionality means here</h2><div class="explainer-grid"><article><h3>Skills that travel</h3><p>A skill helps more when you can show it in more than one setting, not only list it.</p></article><article><h3>Choices you can undo</h3><p>A staged, low-cost start lets you learn before you lock in time or money.</p></article><article><h3>Clear rules</h3><p>Open options cannot make up for missing entry rules, recognition or licences. Check them on the official website.</p></article></div>
    <h2>A worked example (an illustration, not advice)</h2><p>A student compares a 3-year BCA with a 1-year diploma in digital marketing. For the BCA the answers are two routes, skills used in several kinds of work, a fair part recoverable, a small project possible and mostly clear rules, which gives 9 of 15.</p><p>The diploma scores higher on reversibility and testing, because it costs less and can be tried through a small project. The tool does not say which is better. It shows that the BCA needs a staged plan, while the diploma needs a check on whether employers accept it.</p>
    <h2>When to talk to a person</h2><p>Before a costly choice, talk to a teacher, a counsellor or someone who works in the field. For rules and recognition, read the notice on the official website of the exam body or council.</p></section>
    <ToolNextStep toolName="Career Optionality Calculator" audience="professional" />
  </main><Footer />
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .tool-hero{padding:5rem 1.25rem 2rem}.tool-hero h1{max-width:850px}.tool-hero h1 span{color:var(--gold,#e5b84a)}.lead{max-width:760px;font-size:1.15rem;line-height:1.7}.tool-limits{max-width:760px;font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.6;color:var(--muted,#b9c2c1)}.tool-shell{max-width:1050px}.tool-explainer{padding:2rem 1.25rem 5rem}.tool-explainer h2{margin-top:0}.explainer-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:1rem}.explainer-grid article{padding:1rem;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.14);border-radius:14px}.explainer-grid h3{margin-top:0}@media(max-width:720px){.tool-hero{padding-top:3rem}.explainer-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
</style>
